3	//! Offline master-key (KEK) re-wrap: decrypt every KEK-wrapped secret with the
4	//! old master key and re-encrypt it under a new one, in one transaction.
5	//!
6	//! This changes no underlying key material — the same P-256 repo keys, OAuth
7	//! key, JWT secret, Iroh identity, and recovery shares come back out; only the AES-256-GCM
8	//! envelope around each one is replaced. No PLC operation, no firehose event,
9	//! no network effect. It exists so the operator can rotate
10	//! `EZPDS_SIGNING_KEY_MASTER_KEY` (proactively, or after suspected exposure of
11	//! the key alone) without destroying the wrapped keys.
12	//!
13	//! Invoked by the `pds rewrap-master-key` subcommand (`main.rs`) while the
14	//! server is stopped — the single-connection pool means a running server and
15	//! this tool cannot share the DB anyway. Atomicity contract: any blob that
16	//! fails to decrypt with the old key (wrong key, foreign ciphertext) aborts
17	//! the whole transaction, so the DB is always uniformly under exactly one key.
18	
19	use sqlx::SqlitePool;
20	
21	use crate::db::kek::{
22	    get_kek_generation, list_wrapped_secrets, set_kek_generation, update_wrapped_secret,
23	    SecretFamily,
24	};
