2	//
3	// The admin-companion's notification endpoints — the operator-alert analog of
4	// `routes/notifications.rs`:
5	//
6	//   POST /v1/admin/notifications/register    — register this admin device for push
7	//   GET  /v1/admin/notifications/sender-keys — the set of keys to pin
8	//
9	// Auth is `require_admin` (master token OR a device-signed envelope), not `authenticate_access`:
10	// an operator device authenticates *as a device*, never as an account.
11	//
12	// The registration is keyed by the authenticated device id, taken from the credential rather
13	// than the body. That is the whole security property of this route: a device can register a
14	// push key for itself and for nothing else, so a compromised operator device cannot redirect
15	// another device's alerts to itself. It follows that the master token — which authenticates
16	// no particular device — cannot register at all.
