| `agents.rs` | `GET /v1/agents`, `POST /v1/agents/claim-preview`, `POST /v1/agents/{registration_id}/revoke`, `GET /v1/agents/{registration_id}/audit` — per-account agent management for the wallet's "My agents" surface (account-holder routes, not admin ones). Auth is the shared owner guard `auth::guards::authenticate_account_owner` accepting **either** a wallet session token or a full-access OAuth/XRPC access token (the `transfer/complete` dual-credential posture; also the claim-confirm gate's guard); agent-derived and app-password credentials are refused — an agent must never list, preview, audit, or revoke agents, including itself. A registration is operable by the account it acts as **or** by the parent of a sovereign `child` registration (the parent provisions, revokes, and audits its children — a child's own tokens never pass the owner guard, so without the parent arm its audit trail would be readable by no one); unknown and foreign registration ids are a uniform 404 (no cross-account existence oracle). **list** returns each identity's type/issuer/subject/scopes/status plus `lastUsedAt` (newest activity audit event). **claim-preview** answers "what would confirming this `user_code` grant?" — registration type + the operator's current canonicalized `granted_scopes` — so the wallet shows the grant *before* the biometric approval gate; authorization mirrors confirm, every failure shape is the same 404, and it shares the confirm endpoint's rate-limit budget (same code-guessing surface). **revoke** flips `status = 'revoked'` and writes the `revoked` audit event atomically; idempotent (a repeat is 200 with no duplicate event). **audit** pages the V040 trail newest-first with a rowid cursor |
| `notifications.rs` | `POST /v1/notifications/register`, `DELETE /v1/notifications/register/{deviceUuid}`, `GET /v1/notifications/sender-keys` — the account-holder's push surface, authed through `auth::extractors::authenticate_access` (the DPoP-binding seam; `just auth-seam-check` applies). Registration keys on `(did, device_uuid)`, **not** `devices.id` (those rows die at DID promotion), and deliberately does **not** wait on the relay: it stores the row, enqueues the relay registration, and answers `{"status":"pending"}` — this is one of the first calls the wallet makes after promotion, and a third-party relay's uptime does not belong in the onboarding critical path for a feature whose whole failure mode is a missed banner. DELETE is idempotent (`deleted`/`absent`) and drops the relay handle; the DID comes from the credential, so one account can never unregister another's device. `sender-keys` is the re-pin surface (active + retired, never revoked). All three 501 when no relay is configured — the route exists, the instance simply has not opted in |
| `admin_notifications.rs` | `POST /v1/admin/notifications/register`, `GET /v1/admin/notifications/sender-keys` — the operator analog, authed by `require_admin` (an operator device authenticates *as a device*, never as an account). **Registration requires a device-signed request and refuses the master token** with 400: the row is keyed by the device the credential authenticates, so a device can register a push key for itself and nothing else — a credential naming no particular device could otherwise redirect another device's alerts. Reading the key set is a plain admin read, so the master token is accepted there |
| `notification_views.rs` | Shared, handler-free support for the two notification surfaces (routes may not import one another): the enabled-check, the registration field validators (the public key is *decoded*, not merely length-checked — a key we cannot decode now is a device we cannot reach later), and the `sender-keys` response shape. Same non-handler-support-file pattern as `admin_subject_defs.rs` (Functional Core aside from the DB read) |
| `oauth_templates.rs` | Pure HTML rendering helpers (Functional Core, no handler) |
| `oauth_errors.rs` | Shared `OAuthTokenError` — the RFC 6749 §5.2 `{error, error_description}` responder (with the `use_dpop_nonce` header path) used by `oauth_token/` and `oauth_revoke.rs` (Functional Core, no handler) |
